#: ../../source/docs/common-flight-controller-wiring.rst:71
#: 5cd88f6c02a545cdb6ba9811f442a97f
msgid "Motor/Servo Connections"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-flight-controller-wiring.rst:73
#: 4655fa480b09437188fe53576321eb20
msgid "Motor ESCs and/or PWM Servos are attached to the PWM outputs of the autopilot."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-flight-controller-wiring.rst:75
#: f5f612dc23484acba6973f88c3aeae3c
msgid "They are labeled either as MAIN/AUX outputs or just as OUTPUTs. These outputs provide the PWM or Dshot signals for motor ESC or servo control of flight surfaces. They can also be sometimes used as general purpose I/O pins for controlling relays, parachutes, grippers, etc."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-flight-controller-wiring.rst:77
#: 67a5ca683b7e42fbab55e0928a1fc1b2
msgid "Those controllers with MAIN/AUX output labels usually indicate that a IOMCU co-processor is being employed. These provide outputs intended for use as the motor/servo outputs and provide a redundant means of control via RC if the main autopilot fails. The MAIN outputs come from this co-processor, while the AUX designated outputs are controlled directly from the autopilot. Most board level autopilots do not use an IOMCU and have outputs only labeled OUTPUTx or Mx."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-flight-controller-wiring.rst:79
#: b4c5f987d8534cf5b72e9f9be89b7dd1
msgid "This distinction is important, since AUX outputs(and OUTPUTs from autopilots without an IOMCU) can be used as GPIOs as well as PWM or Dshot. While MAIN outputs can only be used for PWM."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-flight-controller-wiring.rst:81
#: 8a55bd4abfc84b72b416d28e1eef84e8
msgid "A few autopilots that do NOT use an IOMCU label their outputs as MAIN, so actually do have the capability of use as GPIOs and/or Dshot ESC control outputs. CUAV V5 Nano and Holybro Pixhawk 4 Mini are examples."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-flight-controller-wiring.rst:83
#: e6bd68729d964c6fbd03390c32df1252
msgid "Often these outputs are provided on 3 pin connector strips supplying or distributing servo power and ground, in addition to the individual output signals. This power is usually provided externally, such as by the ESC or a BEC, although some autopilots provide this power from internal regulators."
msgstr ""

